<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    The operator's window onto crawl state.

    A push connector gets no row on the Microsoft 365 admin centre's connector
    page - that surface belongs to the Graph connector agent, and nothing in this
    repository can change it. sql/20 through sql/25 keep the information that
    page would have shown. This project is where an operator looks at it.

    Razor Pages, and specifically not Web Forms. The user asked about .aspx;
    classic Web Forms is .NET Framework 4.8 only and cannot target the
    net10.0/net9.0 pair the rest of this solution builds for, so it would have
    meant a second runtime on the server and a second thing to patch. Razor Pages
    is the direct successor to the model .aspx people actually want - a markup
    file with a code-behind class, rendered on the server - and it hosts in IIS
    through the ASP.NET Core Module with Windows authentication, which is the
    deployment this estate has.

    No npm, no client build step, no CDN. The network this runs on blocks
    outbound HTTP, so a page that fetches a chart library renders as a broken
    box on the only machine it will ever run on. Everything is server rendered:
    even the trend chart is SVG emitted from the data in Presentation/.

    It references NO other project in this solution, and that is deliberate.
    The dashboard reads a database. It does not push to Graph, it holds no
    credential beyond the app pool's own Windows identity, and dragging PushCore
    in would put the Graph SDK and Azure.Identity into a web tier that has no
    use for either - a larger attack surface and a larger patch obligation for
    nothing. The one thing it shares with the connector is the SQL schema, and
    that is shared as SQL rather than as code.

    Microsoft.Data.SqlClient is at the version Directory.Packages.props decides
    for the whole solution. Two SqlClient versions in one estate is two advisory
    trails to follow; build/Get-OfflinePackages.ps1 restores this graph offline
    and a floating version breaks that.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>$(ConnectorTargetFramework)</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>ConnectorState.Dashboard</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>ConnectorState.Dashboard</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    There is no bundling step and no separate views assembly to keep in sync:
    the .cshtml files compile into this assembly at build, so a deployment is a
    file copy plus an app pool recycle.
  -->

  <!--
    Azure.Identity and Azure.Core used to be listed here too, as PackageReference
    lines to packages this project never calls.

    They had to be. SqlClient depends on Azure.Identity for its AAD
    authentication modes; this project uses none of them - it connects with
    Integrated Security - but the package is in the restore graph regardless, and
    left alone NuGet resolved SqlClient's own floor of 1.11.4 here while every
    other project in the solution carried 1.21.0. Azure.Core split the same way
    one level down: 1.54.0 everywhere else because
    Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets 4.11.0 requires it, and Azure.Identity's
    floor of 1.53.0 here, in the one project with no vault. Two versions of one
    identity library across a solution is what the offline restore set then has
    to carry twice, and what CI's package guard exists to catch.

    Directory.Packages.props does that job now, for transitive arrivals as well
    as direct ones, so the workaround is gone rather than merely moved. If a
    future change reintroduces the split it will show up as two versions in
    build/Get-OfflinePackages.ps1 and fail the guard - which is where it was
    always visible, and now the only place it needs to be.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The same build-time gate every other project here carries. It matters more
    in this one than anywhere else: a web tier is where somebody eventually
    pastes a connection string with a password into appsettings.json, and this
    fails the build when they do. CrawlStateOptions builds the connection string
    from a server and a database name with Integrated Security, so there is
    nothing for that person to paste.
  -->
  <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\build\SecretHygiene.targets" />

</Project>
